Z||000011||jelly2m8||10-31-2001||11:55 PM||||Hey guys,<P> I am here in Vero Beach FL at Devotshka's place relaxing to his great hospitality.<P> Picked up the 88 T-Top coupe this morning in Orlando.<BR> <BR> Looks like the best route is going to be towing it home. The car is running, but has a problem where it doesnt idle steady, and dies when reving it up. I tried a few quick fixes, fuel filter and a TPS sensor. But no go. I have next to no tools, just a few basics I threw together. <BR> So much for being told that the car runs good......<P> Anyhow, Devotshka and I looked it over, and there are a bunch of issues that wouldnt be a problem on a short drive, but would be a problem over a long distance. Other than the motor, the fuel guage, fan motor, the right headlamp motor dont work, the 4 tires arent satisfactory for such a long drive. Theres a bad ball joint on the car, and the rear wheels look way out of alignment. <P> Other than that it should be a good project car, rust free, and t-tops which are C&C installs. The documentation from C&C is surprisingly with the car. They were installed before being deliverd to the Fl dealer. The rubber seals are in good condition, but the felt belt strips (?)at the front and rear edge of the roof panel openings will need to be replaced. The key for the locks, and the end caps are all there, thats a plus.<P> All in all, I am happy with the $300.00 purchase price and what it will cost to get it home. Firstly it is next to impossible to get a completely rust free Fiero where I live, 88's are very few and far between, as is A/C. and untill today, I have never seen a T-Top fiero. So the basics are there, I can build from it.<P> Skitime, I'm not sure on making it to your place in time, I 'm going to have to work around the rental. I'll let you know whats happening when I get in the area. I'll give you a call. Thanks for the inquiery on how I'm doing.<P> I'll leave another update when I get to a computer next.<BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||85SE||11-01-2001||12:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by newfie:<BR><B>I'm thinking about doing the same thing after the winter.  Driving a car from the southern states back here to Newfoundland canada.  What things have to be considered in terms of registration, taxes, duties, insurance that kind of stuff?  I asked this to a few people but can't get a definate answer.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Newfie,  As Jelly noted, you need the RIV.  The long and the short of it on a Fiero seems to be that it's pretty good.  I was reading up on it on their web site and the Fiero needs no mods to come across the border and if you buy one that's 15 years old (so I guess that now includes some 87's) there's something about duties being lower - I can't remember and it's getting late  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Check the RIV out at <A HREF="http://www.riv.ca" TARGET=_blank>http://www.riv.ca</A> <P><BR>I belive that Slammed and family have imported a number of cars into Canada and have some great insights into the process.  Perhaps he's lurking around here?  I recall him saying once that it was far easier to pay a broker $150 to take care of it than deal with the headaches yourself.<P>I also talked once with (IIRC) David Berquiest (sp?) from BC.  He brought an 85GT across the border and he had a bad experience as the car was a bit of a project car - most of the work had to be done before it was allowed across the border  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Cheers - Andrew - yeah, the guy in the same faculty as you - currently vacationing - er, on workterm - in Ottawa<BR><P>------------------<BR>Andrew Menchions - 85SE - AKA Fieroless in Newfoundland||||reg||14
